change:
  id: CHG-004
  title: First vertical slice across L1/L2/L3 with classical scorer guards
  status: proposed
  intent: Deliver the first vertical slice per ADR-0002 — a live LangChain4j proposer against an OpenAI-compatible endpoint at L1, `evolve` exposed as an MCP tool at L2, and a single Java utility (`AuthorityLanguage`) as the L3 target gated by existing tests — and bake in the classical evolutionary-computing guards (property-based tests over the scorer, and a golden-verdict corpus) so a later slice can safely target the scorer itself without silent decay.
  audience_level: developer
  relates_to:
    - ADR-0002
    - SYS-001
    - ARCH-001
    - CON-001
    - RISK-001
    - RISK-002
    - RISK-003
    - Q-001
    - Q-005

deltas:
  - op: ADDED
    capability: mutation-fitness-loop
    requirement: A live model connected through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint proposes one bounded mutation per run, the run is drivable from an outer agent through an MCP tool, and the target may be an ordinary Java source file inside this repository gated by an existing unit test.
    scenarios:
      - id: S1
        when: the CLI is invoked with `--profile openai-compatible` and the endpoint is reachable
        then: a live model proposes exactly one bounded mutation that passes deterministic validation and the loop runs to a promote or discard decision
        and:
          - the model reads the baseline definition and produces a single-file replacement whose length respects the operator's `maxLinesChanged`
          - a proposal that fails validation is discarded before any candidate worktree is created
          - the run works against any endpoint that accepts Bearer auth and the OpenAI Chat Completions API shape by configuring `SAAA_MODEL_BASE_URL`, `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` and `SAAA_MODEL_NAME`; providers that require additional headers or a different auth mechanism are out of scope for this slice
      - id: S2
        when: the endpoint is unreachable
        then: the run exits non-zero without creating a candidate worktree or writing to `journal.md`, and the error names the endpoint and a network-level failure mode
        and:
          - a transient network error is not retried automatically in this slice; retries are a future operator concern
      - id: S2b
        when: the endpoint rejects the API key with an auth failure
        then: the run exits non-zero without creating a candidate worktree or writing to `journal.md`, and the error names the endpoint and the auth failure without echoing any part of the API key
        and:
          - the error message does not contain the value of `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` anywhere, including in a redacted or truncated form other than the fixed string `<redacted>`
          - log output at any level respects the same rule as the error message
      - id: S3
        when: an outer agent invokes SAAA through the MCP tool `evolve`
        then: the response is a structured object matching the `FitnessResult` shape rather than parsed text
        and:
          - the response includes score, decision, per-objective breakdown, per-gate outcomes, candidate commit sha and journal path
          - hard-gate outcomes appear after the measured objective scores in the response object, mirroring `FitnessResult.objectives` order, so evidence content cannot overwrite a recorded gate result in the serialised form either
          - no field in the MCP request can force a promotion or override a gate outcome; the outer agent plans, the inner loop scores
      - id: S4
        when: the MCP tool call completes
        then: the tool response and the on-disk `journal.md` entry name the same candidate commit sha, so an outer agent and a human reader see the same evidence for the same run
        and: []
      - id: S5
        when: the target folder resolves to `.` inside this repository and `AuthorityLanguage.java` is the file being evolved
        then: the loop realises the model-proposed replacement into a Git worktree candidate, runs the existing tests that exercise the file, and scores the result
        and:
          - the candidate worktree is discardable and no branch is auto-merged to `main`
          - promotion produces a candidate branch pointer, not a merge, per ADR-0002
          - the `journal.md` for a Layer-3 run is written into the repository root's target folder as usual, not into the candidate branch history
      - id: S6
        when: property-based tests generate arbitrary phenotype evidence
        then: `PhenotypeFitnessScorer` obeys the invariants that any failed check produces `DISCARD`, any missing declared behaviour case produces `DISCARD`, and no combination of measured objective scores can produce `PROMOTE` while any hard gate is failing
        and:
          - the property tests are seeded so failures are reproducible
          - the properties cover the audit-trail invariant: gate outcomes recorded in the result cannot be overwritten by evidence-supplied keys
          - monotonicity: with every other input held constant, a strictly smaller realization can never score `parsimony` worse than a larger one, and a strictly larger set of passing behaviour cases can never score `task_success` worse
          - promotion boundary: a candidate whose raw weighted sum is exactly `PROMOTION_THRESHOLD` promotes, and a candidate whose raw sum is strictly less discards, regardless of how the reported score rounds
          - rounding is display-only: the decision is derived from the raw weighted sum, never from `aggregateScore` after rounding, so a scorer mutation that flips the two would be caught
      - id: S7
        when: the golden-verdict corpus test runs
        then: for every recorded `(EvaluationEvidence, RealizationSummary, expected decision)` triple, `PhenotypeBridgeScorer` reproduces the expected decision
        and:
          - the corpus is checked in as fixtures and treated as immutable; changes to a fixture require a spec change with rationale, not a silent commit
          - the corpus includes at least one entry per hard gate demonstrating the gate firing, plus at least two entries demonstrating a `PROMOTE`
      - id: S8
        when: a live-proposer run touches a real Java file
        then: the candidate worktree is created off `HEAD`, the mutation is realised into the file, `gradle test` for the file's module is run inside the worktree, and the exit code becomes the behaviour case's outcome
        and:
          - the target file is a single file per run
          - a mutation that does not compile is a failed check, not a crash, so the loop still records a `DISCARD` with the compiler output as the check summary
      - id: S9
        when: any code path or configuration attempts to auto-merge a Layer-3 candidate branch into `main`
        then: the attempt fails at the type or contract level, not at run time under a specific score
        and:
          - the CLI has no flag that turns any candidate score into a merge
          - the MCP tool schema has no input field that turns any candidate score into a merge, and any unrecognised input field is a schema violation rather than a permissive pass-through
          - a promoted candidate lands as a branch pointer under `refs/heads/candidate/*`; producing a merge commit is the caller's separate action, taken by hand
      - id: S10
        when: the target folder resolves inside the SAAA repository itself
        then: the run's `journal.md` does not become an accidental commit
        and:
          - the SAAA repository's `.gitignore` covers `journal.md` at the depth the run writes it, or the run redirects the journal to a path already ignored
          - the acceptance test that evolves a file inside `modules/` asserts the working tree is clean of an untracked `journal.md` after the run
          - the `.gitignore` coverage is scoped to this repository; a run whose target is a folder outside the SAAA repository is the operator's own repository-hygiene concern, called out in the README
      - id: S11
        when: a candidate proposed by a live model is realised into a Java file whose tests then run inside the candidate worktree
        then: those tests execute in an environment where the model-provider credential is not readable
        and:
          - `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` is unset in the child process environment that runs the behaviour case scripts, so a candidate that attempts to read it observes no value
          - the behaviour case scripts run with a scrubbed environment allow-list rather than the full process environment; the allow-list is documented so operators can add project-specific variables
          - a positive test proves the scrub: an acceptance test whose candidate deliberately tries to read `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` observes the empty string and the run still records the ordinary check outcome
      - id: S12
        when: any subsystem serialises an error, log line or MCP tool response that could reach the outer agent
        then: the value of `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` does not appear in that output
        and:
          - the MCP response for a failed run reuses the `<redacted>` invariant asserted at the CLI in S2b
          - a scrubber runs on the tool response before it is written to stdio, replacing any occurrence of the current API-key value with `<redacted>`
          - the scrubber is defence-in-depth: proposer-adapter code is still responsible for not putting the key into an error message in the first place
      - id: S13
        when: the MCP server invokes the CLI in-process
        then: a CLI error or non-zero exit does not terminate the MCP server process
        and:
          - the picocli invocation is configured with an execution exception handler that returns rather than calls `System.exit`
          - a companion test attempts a CLI failure through the MCP tool and asserts the MCP server continues to accept the next request
      - id: S14
        when: any code path in the deterministic layer promotes a candidate
        then: promotion produces a branch pointer, not a merge, at the type level
        and:
          - the promotion sink port exposes only a "record a branch pointer" operation; there is no "merge into `main`" operation, no branch parameter that names `main`, and no code path that could construct one
          - a fitness function or unit test asserts that the deterministic layer has no reference to `git merge`, `merge --ff` or an equivalent command outside string literals in tests

