tasks:
  - id: T1
    slice: add an OpenAI-compatible LangChain4j chat-model wiring with configurable `SAAA_MODEL_BASE_URL`, `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY`, `SAAA_MODEL_NAME`, kept inside `modules/adapters/langchain4j`
    status: pending
    note: uses `OpenAiChatModel` with a custom `baseUrl` so OpenAI, NeuralWatt, together.ai, groq and Ollama's `/v1` shim all fit one adapter; provider-neutral so credentials are never seen by the deterministic layer
  - id: T2
    slice: implement `OpenAiCompatibleMutationProposer` behind the existing `MutationProposer` port, producing exactly one `Mutation` whose `patch` is a whole-file replacement whose *diff* against the baseline respects the operator's `maxLinesChanged` bound
    status: pending
    note: the bound is on the diff size, not the emitted-content length; a whole-file replacement of the same total length can still change every line and would exceed `maxLinesChanged`; replies whose diff violates bounds are dropped before validation; parsing failures are surfaced as run errors, not silent fall-throughs; the adapter must not read scorer or gate state; the exact prompt and the raw model response are logged into the candidate's `.saaa/candidates/<id>.toon` bookkeeping so a later run or a scorer-as-target audit can reproduce them from the commit
  - id: T2b
    slice: extend the candidate bookkeeping TOON written by `GitCandidateWorkspace` to include the proposer id, prompt digest, and raw response for live-model runs; the fixture proposer records only its file path
    status: pending
    note: prerequisite for later audits that need to distinguish "the scorer changed its mind" from "the model produced a different proposal"
  - id: T3
    slice: register the live proposer as `openai-compatible` in `ProposerProfileRegistry` so `--profile openai-compatible` is a first-class choice alongside `fixture`
    status: pending
  - id: T4
    slice: add a new `mcp` package inside `modules/adapters` exposing an MCP server that wraps the existing CLI's `evolve` command as an MCP tool, with input schema, output schema and a stdio transport that any MCP client can drive
    status: pending
    note: placed inside `modules/adapters` per the `invariant_named_module_layout` decision in `PROJECT_PROFILE.toon`; the MCP module is an adapter, must not import model-provider libraries, and treats the picocli CLI class as the boundary rather than reimplementing the loop; invokes the CLI in-process via its picocli command class rather than as a subprocess, so a JVM is not started per MCP call; the picocli invocation is configured with an `IExecutionExceptionHandler` and an `IExitCodeExceptionMapper` so a CLI failure returns a non-zero code without calling `System.exit`, per S13
  - id: T5
    slice: define the MCP tool response contract as a stable JSON serialisation of `FitnessResult` where gate outcomes are written after measured objective scores so the audit-trail invariant survives serialisation, `aggregateScore` is emitted both as the raw double under `aggregateScore` and as a two-decimal string under `aggregateScoreDisplay`, `journalPath` is an absolute path so an outer agent process with a different cwd can still read it, and per-field string values are individually capped (`checkSummary <= 4000 chars`, error message `<= 8000 chars`) with the total serialised response also capped so a hostile candidate cannot DoS the stdio transport
    status: pending
  - id: T6
    slice: enforce that MCP input can never carry a field that overrides a gate outcome, forces a promotion, enables an auto-merge, or transports credentials; the schema is closed (unknown keys are a schema violation) and any candidate input field must resolve to an existing CLI flag with the same meaning
    status: pending
    note: the load-bearing rule from ADR-0002 lives here at the wire; a permissive schema would reintroduce the original problem one level up; credentials arrive only via the MCP server's environment, never as tool arguments
  - id: T6b
    slice: add a response scrubber to the MCP output pipeline that replaces any occurrence of the current `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` value (and known transport headers, e.g. `Authorization: Bearer …`) with `<redacted>` before the response reaches stdio, per S12
    status: pending
    note: defence-in-depth against the proposer adapter, LangChain4j, or a serialisation library echoing a header or body containing the key; the primary defence is still "don't put it into the error in the first place"
  - id: T7
    slice: add jqwik property-based tests over `PhenotypeFitnessScorer` covering the invariants named in S6; run inside the existing `:deterministic:test` task
    status: pending
    note: guardrail against future scorer-as-target slices; the properties describe intent rather than specific cases so a mutation cannot accidentally satisfy them while breaking meaning
  - id: T8
    slice: check in a golden-verdict corpus as immutable TOON fixtures under `modules/deterministic/src/test/resources/golden-corpus/` and a test that iterates them
    status: completed
    note: |
      TOON per the repository's `state_and_contracts` structured-data rule; coverage floor is
      (a) one entry per hard gate demonstrating the gate firing, (b) two `PROMOTE` entries with
      different objective profiles, (c) one entry that scores exactly `PROMOTION_THRESHOLD` and
      promotes, (d) one entry that scores just under `PROMOTION_THRESHOLD` and discards, and
      (e) at least one entry captured from a real acceptance run so the corpus is grounded in
      observed evidence and not only in constructed cases; changes to a fixture require a spec
      change with rationale rather than a silent commit.

      Shipped state: the corpus is nine entries in
      `modules/deterministic/src/test/java/com/dreamthought/saaa/deterministic/GoldenCorpus.java`
      as programmatic Java constants rather than TOON files, and
      `PhenotypeGoldenVerdictCorpusTest` iterates them via `@ParameterizedTest`. The Java
      constant format is a recorded deviation from the TOON pin — there is no Java TOON reader
      yet — scoped to this file and documented in `docs/structured-data.md`; it retires when
      `CHG-002` task `T3d` adds the envelope reader, at which point the entries migrate to
      resource files.

      Coverage: three of four hard gates fire through the bridge in the corpus
      (`checks`, `required_behavior_cases`, `non_empty_realization`); the fourth
      (`required_objective_scores`) cannot fire through the bridge because the bridge always
      produces all five objective values, so it is covered instead by
      `PhenotypeFitnessScorerTest` and property `noObjectiveCombinationCanPromoteWhileAGateFails`
      in `PhenotypeFitnessScorerPropertyTest`. The floor also picks up two entries at the
      promotion boundary (one PROMOTE at exactly 0.80, one DISCARD at 0.7976 that displays as
      0.80 — the latter also covers property `P8` at the corpus level) and one entry captured
      from the CHG-003 fixture `toy-workflow` acceptance run.
  - id: T9
    slice: make `CommandCheckRunner` treat a compiler failure inside the candidate as a failed check with the compiler output as the summary, so a non-compiling mutation is discarded rather than crashing the run
    status: pending
    note: independently valuable for any target that could fail to compile, and a prerequisite before any Layer-3 target
  - id: T9b
    slice: run behaviour case scripts inside the candidate worktree with a scrubbed environment allow-list; the default allow-list is `PATH`, `HOME`, `LANG`, `LC_*` and `JAVA_HOME`; `SAAA_MODEL_*` and any other model-provider credentials are never present in the child environment, per S11
    status: pending
    note: prevents a candidate whose realised code deliberately or accidentally reads a credential from the process environment; operators can extend the allow-list per project through a documented mechanism; the acceptance test proves the scrub with a candidate that tries to read `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` and observes the empty string
  - id: T9c
    slice: mark the promotion sink port as branch-pointer-only at the type level; the port exposes one operation that records a candidate ref under `refs/heads/candidate/*`, and no method has a target-branch parameter that could name `main`; add a source-scanning fitness function that fails the build if the deterministic layer references `git merge` outside test string literals, per S14
    status: pending
    note: closes the "no code path merges" invariant at compile time rather than by review; a later feature that actually needs a merge would have to add a new port and a new decision, both visible in review
  - id: T10
    slice: add the Layer-3 acceptance test evolving `modules/deterministic/src/main/java/com/dreamthought/saaa/deterministic/AuthorityLanguage.java` in a scratch worktree, gated by the existing `BoundedMutationValidator` tests
    status: pending
    note: the test asserts that a promotion produces a candidate branch pointer under `refs/heads/candidate/*` and no merge to `main`; a companion acceptance test asserts the CLI has no flag that turns any score into a merge; the blast-radius constraints from ADR-0002 are enforced by test rather than by convention
  - id: T10b
    slice: extend `.gitignore` to cover `journal.md` at every path a Layer-3 run could write it, and add an acceptance test that evolves a file inside `modules/` and asserts the working tree is clean of an untracked `journal.md` after the run
    status: pending
    note: without this, running SAAA against a target inside the SAAA repo can produce an accidental `journal.md` commit; the acceptance test would catch a regression that stops the gitignore matching
  - id: T11
    slice: update `README.md`, `docs/wiki/development.md` and `docs/wiki/domain.md` to describe the `openai-compatible` profile, the MCP tool surface, the classical guards, the Layer-3 constraints, and the "endpoint accepts Bearer auth and the OpenAI Chat Completions API shape" scope
    status: pending
  - id: T12
    slice: update `PROJECT_PROFILE.toon` with any new decisions (endpoint-neutral provider adapter constrained to Bearer + Chat Completions shape, MCP as first L2 surface with a closed schema, in-process CLI invocation, staged-hybrid guardrails) and `HANDOFF.toon` on merge; flip `Q-001` from `open` to `answered-by-CHG-004` and add a brief rationale linking to this change
    status: pending

non_goals:
  - population — more than one candidate per generation stays a foundation slice per ADR-0002
  - conceptual crossover — same
  - scorer-as-mutation-target — deferred until the guards this slice adds have real evidence of holding, per the staged hybrid; a later slice may attempt it
  - AST-aware realisation — whole-file text replacement stays the operator model; a mutation the model cannot express as a whole-file rewrite is out of scope this slice
  - mutation-testing sweep (PIT or similar) — belongs with the scorer-as-target slice, not this one
  - retry policies for transient endpoint failures — a future operator concern
  - a full outer agentic loop of our own — this slice exposes SAAA to an outer agent; building the outer agent is the caller's job
  - promoted refs to `refs/heads/promoted/*` from `CHG-002 T5` — a candidate branch pointer is enough for this slice and does not depend on that scheme
  - multiple L3 target files per run — one file per candidate per ADR-0002 blast-radius constraints
  - auto-merge of any Layer-3 candidate under any score — hard rule; no CLI flag or MCP input weakens this
  - MCP discovery / listing tools (e.g. `list-runs`, `list-behaviour-cases`, `read-journal`) — the outer agent knows behaviour case names and target paths out-of-band for this slice; discovery is a follow-on
  - MCP tool arguments carrying credentials — credentials arrive only via the MCP server's environment; the schema refuses to accept them as inputs
  - subprocess-per-invocation MCP transport — the MCP server invokes the CLI's picocli class in-process to avoid JVM startup per call
  - support for providers requiring auth mechanisms beyond Bearer or API shapes beyond OpenAI Chat Completions — a subsequent adapter would handle those
  - proposer diversity knobs (temperature, top-p, nucleus sampling) — this slice runs one candidate per invocation; per-profile diversity settings land with the population slice that actually needs them
  - full sandboxing of executed candidate Java (SecurityManager, containerisation, seccomp) — the environment-scrub in T9b closes the credential-leak vector for this slice; general sandboxing is a much larger investment and is not part of the vertical demonstration
  - protecting a target folder outside the SAAA repository from `journal.md` clutter — the operator's own repository hygiene applies there; the README documents the write location so it is not surprising

risks:
  - a live model can produce output that parses cleanly but is semantically nonsense; `BoundedMutationValidator` and the deterministic scorer are the only guards; expect noisy discards on early runs
  - the MCP tool contract stabilises now, before we know exactly what an outer agent will want; the schema may need extension in a follow-up slice
  - property-based tests are only as strong as the invariants they encode; missing an invariant hides scorer decay just as effectively as no property tests at all, so the invariant list must be reviewed as part of this change rather than accepted implicitly
  - the golden-verdict corpus is only as strong as its coverage; a scorer mutation that changes behaviour on evidence shapes absent from the corpus will not be caught
  - environment-variable configuration for the endpoint means a misconfigured endpoint fails at run time rather than at build time; documentation and pre-flight need to make this obvious
  - `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` in the environment is a credential in an environment variable; agents driving this via MCP must respect that and not echo it in any tool response
  - `AuthorityLanguage` is small; a mutation that removes the regex entirely may still pass compilation and only fail one test, which reads as an unrealistically easy `DISCARD`; the acceptance test cannot force the live model to try a specific mutation shape, so the concrete constraint is that the fixture-driven L3 acceptance test asserts at least one `PROMOTE` and one `DISCARD` across a small deterministic run set, keeping the "loop actually differentiates" claim honest even when the live model is not exercised in CI
  - the MCP module adds a new dependency surface and a new transport (stdio); expect operational rough edges on early integrations
  - LangChain4j provider-neutral typed-service coverage exists today, but running against a real endpoint in CI requires either recorded responses or a locally reachable model; the integration test must not require live secrets to pass
  - the L3 target executes arbitrary Java produced by a live model inside the same JVM as the test process; the environment-scrub in T9b closes the credential-leak vector but does not sandbox the code otherwise; a candidate that opens a network connection, writes outside the worktree, or exhausts memory is still able to; the acceptance test suite is expected to run under an operator willing to accept that trust boundary, and full sandboxing is a named non-goal
  - concurrent MCP `evolve` invocations against the same target folder collide on `.worktrees/candidate-*` naming (RISK-003); the MCP surface makes this trivial where the CLI required user cooperation, but the folding of RISK-003 into the population slice already covers it
  - the response scrubber in T6b protects against an unhandled error trace leaking the current `SAAA_MODEL_API_KEY` value, but cannot protect against a leak of an out-of-band credential the operator has also set; the primary defence remains that the proposer adapter does not put credentials into error messages
  - property tests may find counterexamples to the monotonicity or boundary invariants that reflect legitimate scoring decisions rather than defects; the process is to convert each counterexample into a named example test with a rationale, or to tighten the property, before shipping
